  "content": {
    "title": "U.S. Government's \"Illegal\" Shake-Up Brings Changes For National Park Service Employees",
    "text": "On Thursday, April 2, U.S. Secretary of the Interior Doug Burgum sent an email to the U.S. Interior Department's 70,000 workers, including National Park Service (NPS) employees, announcing \"an important step.\"Although specific implementation plans remain confidential, a recent press release mentions that more NPS workers will soon be reassigned to visitor-facing roles. Park advocacy groups are now sounding the alarm, claiming that NPS sites will be negatively affected by this shift.U.S. Government's Latest Move Will Reassign More NPS Employees To Visitor-Facing RolesJoseph Sohm/ShutterstockAccording to the email sent to NPS workers, the Interior Department is about to take \"an important step forward in strengthening service to the American people.\" The press release published on April 2 also announces key changes for NPS workers.\"Highlights of this initiative include aligning more National Park Service positions to visitor‑facing roles, strengthening support for tribal nations and tribal justice, and modernizing key processes such as permitting by eliminating redundant layers,\" the press release states.The email also claims that the initiative reflects \"a commitment to delivering faster, more reliable service to the public.\" In addition, the new measures will sharpen focus on \"core water and power missions\" while \"reducing administrative burdens and improving internal operations to ensure team members have the tools and support needed to provide fast, reliable service to the public, our partners and stakeholders.\"The press release also mentions the advancement of responsible land and wildlife management across the country and the acceleration of the delivery of \"high-quality science.\"However, advocacy groups remain skeptical, especially on this last point, claiming that assigning more NPS workers to visitor-facing roles may result in fewer people working for the Natural Resource Stewardship and Science Directorate, something that may be illegal under the National Parks Omnibus Management Act.This Staff Shift May Violate The National Parks Omnibus Management ActThe Natural Resource Stewardship and Science Directorate (NRSS) provides \"scientific, technical, and administrative support to national parks for the management of natural resources.\" It aims to develop, utilize, and distribute tools to help the NPS fulfill its core mission: protecting NPS sites so that they remain unimpaired for future generations.Because of this, reducing the NRSS workforce may be illegal under the National Parks Omnibus Management Act of 1998, which directs the Secretary of the Interior to \"assure that management of NPS units is enhanced by the availability and utilization of a broad program of the highest quality science and information.\"This is not the first time the U.S. Government has been accused of violating the law. In 2025, former NPS workers accused the Trump administration of violating the Organic Act and the Redwood Act by keeping national parks open during the federal shutdown.By shifting employees from natural resource management positions to visitor-facing roles, the Trump administration may also be going against the Organic Act.The Organic Act Claims The NPS's Main Aim Is Conservationvia ShutterstockIn 1916, President Woodrow Wilson signed the Organic Act, officially establishing the NPS, whose aim was to protect the 35 national parks then in existence, as well as those yet to be established.\"The service thus established shall promote and regulate the use of the Federal areas known as national parks, monuments, and reservations hereinafter specified by such means and measures as conform to the fundamental purpose of the said parks, monuments, and reservations, which purpose is to conserve the scenery and the natural and historic objects and the wild life therein and to provide for the enjoyment of the same in such manner and by such means as will leave them unimpaired for the enjoyment of future generations,\" states the Organic Act.While ensuring national parks across the country remain safe and provide services for visitors is crucial, the NPS's main aim remains to preserve these sites for future generations, which may be harder to achieve if most workers are focused on visitor-facing roles.Advocacy groups are now openly criticizing the Interior Department's latest initiative, arguing that it runs counter to the very purpose for which the NPS was created.Park Advocacy Groups Are Criticizing The Staff ShiftShutterstockEmily Douce, of the National Park Conservation Association, said this move will worsen an already critical situation, since the NPS is already struggling with the loss of about 24% of its permanent staff in 2025.\"This latest attempt to push park staff out and prioritize visitor-facing roles at all costs runs counter to the very mission the Park Service was created to uphold and the natural and cultural resources it is mandated to protect,\" said Emily Douce in a press release.Similarly, Gerry James, deputy director of the Sierra Club's Outdoors For All campaign, said that, contrary to what the Interior Department claims, this is not an improvement for the NPS, a claim aligning with survey data indicating that 98% of NPS workers don't trust U.S. government leaders.\"If Interior's initiative were really about serving the public better, we would see proposals for stronger staffing, deeper investment, and more support for the people who protect our public lands and welcome people into them,\" Gerry James declared in a statement.While details haven't been made public yet, the NPS regional directors have been summoned to Washington next week to be given more information about these upcoming changes. At the moment, it remains unclear whether Natural Resource Stewardship and Science Directorate employees will be reassigned to visitor-facing roles and how this will affect NPS sites across the country.",
    "word_count": 885,
    "extraction_method": "readability"
  },
